# Defect 314, 2026-10-07, the array beside its string. Symptom: an array
# released once more than it was held ran on in silence wherever the
# allocator wrote a pointer over the freed block's first word, which the
# runtime read as its count: on this Mac at 1, 4 and 32 elements (exit 0,
# the block freed twice), measured at `dad2da47`; the Windows heap stopped it
# as heap corruption with no word of the runtime's. Cause: the count, the
# only thing the check asked, is the allocator's once the block is given
# back. Now the runtime sets the element descriptor, which a live array
# always has, to none before the block goes, and a release finding none is
# refused by name, as a count of 0 already was (`runtime/parts/array.c`).
#
# The array is the C's own, made through the runtime and released twice by a
# function of the program's own header: a program cannot hand C an array's
# block, and the compiler never releases one twice. AddressSanitizer names
# the read of the freed block.

extern "fixedbugs-314-an-array-released-twice-is-named.h"
    function release_twice(elements: i64)

function main()
    print("four elements, released twice")
    release_twice(elements: 4)
